        <p>Nuwara Eliya is Sri Lanka at its most surprising — a hill station at 1,868 metres where Tudor red-brick buildings, a gentlemen's golf club, a horse-racing track and manicured hedges create the uncanny impression of a fragment of the English countryside dropped into the Sri Lankan cloud forest. The British planted tea on these slopes from the 1860s and built a retreat from the coastal heat; today the town is as much a living museum of colonial atmosphere as it is a gateway to Sri Lanka's finest tea country. The air is crisp (averaging 16°C), and after weeks on the coast it genuinely feels like a different climate zone.</p>

        <h2>The Colonial Town — What to See</h2>

        <h3>The Post Office (1894)</h3>
        <p>The most photographed building in Nuwara Eliya — a striking two-storey Tudor structure built from vivid red bricks with a clock spire. One of the oldest still-operating post offices in Sri Lanka. Worth visiting just to post a postcard from "Little England."</p>

        <h3>The Grand Hotel</h3>
        <p>Originally built in 1828 as the private residence of British Governor Sir Edward Barnes, this Elizabethan-style manor has white gables, half-timbered walls and immaculate lawns. Now a 5-star hotel, it remains one of Sri Lanka's great colonial buildings. Even non-guests can walk the grounds — the verandahs and gardens are time-capsule perfect. Afternoon tea here is an experience.</p>

        <h3>The Hill Club (1876)</h3>
        <p>Built from granite blocks by British planters, The Hill Club still operates as a private gentlemen's club with billiard rooms, a formal dining room (jacket and tie required at dinner) and a trophy room that looks unchanged since 1910. Non-members can dine here by arrangement — the mutton chops and school-dinner puddings are completely deliberate. One of Sri Lanka's most atmospheric places for a meal.</p>

        <h3>Nuwara Eliya Golf Club (est. 1889)</h3>
        <p>One of the oldest golf clubs in Asia, set on a scenic highland course surrounded by tea estates. Visitors can play with proof of handicap. The clubhouse itself — all dark wood and framed portraits — is worth poking your head into even if you don't play.</p>

        <h3>The April Races</h3>
        <p>If your timing allows, the Royal Turf Club's April Races (held during Sinhala and Tamil New Year week, usually around 13–14 April) are Sri Lanka's most glamorous annual event. The Colombo elite descend in force, hotels book up six months ahead, and the racing is secondary to the spectacle. An extraordinary throwback to colonial social life.</p>

        <h2>Hakgala Botanical Gardens</h2>
        <p>Eight kilometres from town on the slopes beneath Hakgala Rock, these are Sri Lanka's second-largest botanical gardens and one of the highest-altitude gardens in the world. Established in 1861, the gardens hold some of Sri Lanka's very first experimental tea bushes — planted when botanists needed an alternative crop after an earlier experiment collapsed — making this one of the birthplaces of the Ceylon Tea industry.</p>

        <p>What's special for visitors: because of the cool highland climate, Hakgala is one of the only places in South Asia where English cottage flowers grow alongside prehistoric tree ferns. The Rose Garden has over 300 varieties (best March–May), the century-old Bulb Garden is one of the finest in Asia, and the towering tree fern section feels genuinely primeval.</p>

        <p>There's also a Ramayana connection — the area around Hakgala is believed in Hindu tradition to be the <em>Ashoka Vatika</em>, the garden where Ravana kept Sita captive. A statue of Sita marks the spot. Combined with the nearby Seetha Amman Temple (500m away), this makes for a good half-day loop.</p>

        <p><strong>Practical:</strong> Open 7:30am–5:30pm daily. Entry ~LKR 1,500 (foreigners). Allow 1.5–2 hours.</p>

        <h2>Tea Factory Tours</h2>
        <p>Nuwara Eliya's high altitude produces Ceylon's finest teas — bright, brisk and distinctively aromatic. Pedro Tea Estate and Damro Labookellie are the most visitor-friendly factories: both show the full process from leaf to cup (withering, rolling, oxidation, firing, grading) and end with a tasting session. Walking through the picking fields — Tamil estate workers in brightly coloured saris against a sea of vivid green — is one of the iconic Sri Lanka images.</p>

        <h2>Victoria Park & Gregory Lake</h2>
        <p><strong>Victoria Park</strong> is a beautifully maintained public garden in the town centre, known for birdwatching (Indian blue robin, Kashmir flycatcher). Worth an hour in the morning. <strong>Gregory Lake</strong>, at the eastern edge of town, has a promenade for evening strolls, pedal boats on weekends, and the classic backdrop of tea hills and colonial buildings — especially photogenic at dusk.</p>

        <h3>Seetha Amman Temple</h3>
        <p>A colourful Hindu temple between town and Hakgala, marking the site where Ravana is said to have held Sita. Footprints in the rock are attributed to Hanuman. A Ramayana pilgrimage site visited by large numbers of Indian tourists — fascinating for the energy of active religious devotion at a scenic highland location.</p>

